data.content:
## Breaking Change: Cosmos DB Provider Sync API Removal

Starting with **EF Core 9** (.NET 9), the Cosmos DB provider **blocked synchronous operations by default** because the underlying Cosmos SDK is async-only. The "sync-over-async" opt-in (e.g., configuring the warning to not throw) let you keep sync calls temporarily.

In **.NET 11**, the expectation on this trajectory is that the **sync-over-async opt-in is fully removed** — meaning all four of your methods will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime with no workaround.

### What breaks

| Sync call | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory.
- All callers up the chain must also become `async`/`await` (this propagates to controllers, etc.).
- This only affects the **Cosmos DB provider**. The relational providers (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, etc.) still support sync APIs.

> ⚠️ I don't have confirmed .NET 11 release notes (it's not yet released). Verify the exact timeline in the [EF Core breaking changes docs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/what-is-new/) when the preview drops.
